# Skyfield Snapshot Cross-Check

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:eph_core, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
    {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
    # Embeds CPython in this BEAM — no System.cmd / local venv required.
    {:pythonx, "~> 0.4"}
  ],
  config: [
    eph_core: [
      # Planetary DE440s only. Avoid config_path: :eph_core here — that loads
      # config.exs asteroid kernels before SPK.Server starts.
      kernel_base_dir: Path.expand("../../priv", __DIR__),
      spk_kernels: ["de440s.bsp"]
    ]
  ]
)

Logger.configure(level: :warning)
```

## Overview

One-shot credibility check: EphCore `SnapshotPipeline.observe/4` and
`ApparentPlace.look/2` vs Skyfield, in-process via
[Pythonx](https://hexdocs.pm/pythonx) (`uv`-managed CPython + Skyfield).

**Timing protocol** (so the numbers mean something):

1. Install Python + Skyfield once (`uv_init` — first run can take a minute).
2. **Warm** both engines (load `de440s.bsp`, touch caches) — not timed.
3. Time matched physics on both sides: Skyfield's per-body loop already runs
   `.apparent().altaz()` inside its timed pass, so Elixir's timed pass runs
   `observe/4` **and** `ApparentPlace.look/2` per body together — not
   `observe/4` alone. Reported at single-core (fair vs Skyfield's sequential
   loop) and at Elixir's default multi-core fan-out (a separate, labeled
   concurrency-bonus row — not folded into the "language speed" ratio).

Still a **credibility check**, not a race. For automated regression gates use
`mix eph.snapshot_sanity_check` (dev/test only). Full-year rise/set parity is
demonstrated in [`01_almanac.livemd`](01_almanac.livemd).

Pipeline stage tour (no Python): [`01_snapshot_pipeline.livemd`](../01_observe_pipeline/01_snapshot_pipeline.livemd).

**How to open:** Install [Livebook](https://livebook.dev/), open this `.livemd`,
evaluate the setup cell. Kernels must already be under `eph_core/priv/ephemeris/`
(run `mix eph.download_kernels` in the repo first; the setup cell will not start without baseline files).

## Helpers

```elixir
# Kino.Input.utc_datetime/2 reads as NaiveDateTime (UTC) or DateTime depending on version.
read_utc = fn input ->
  case Kino.Input.read(input) do
    %DateTime{} = dt ->
      DateTime.truncate(dt, :second)

    %NaiveDateTime{} = ndt ->
      ndt |> DateTime.from_naive!("Etc/UTC") |> DateTime.truncate(:second)
  end
end

:ok
```

## Ready check

```elixir
priv = EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.base_dir()
spk = Path.join([priv, "ephemeris", "spk", "de440s.bsp"])

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**EphCore** `#{Application.spec(:eph_core, :vsn)}` · priv `#{priv}`

SPK `de440s.bsp`: #{if File.exists?(spk), do: "✅ present", else: "❌ missing — run `mix eph.download_kernels`"}
""")
```

## 1 — Observer & epoch

Minneapolis defaults — edit and re-evaluate.

```elixir
lat_in = Kino.Input.number("Latitude (°)", default: 44.9778)
lon_in = Kino.Input.number("Longitude (°)", default: -93.2650)
height_in = Kino.Input.number("Height (m)", default: 250)
utc_in = Kino.Input.utc_datetime("UTC", default: ~N[2026-07-21 17:00:00])

Kino.Layout.grid([lat_in, lon_in, height_in, utc_in], columns: 2)
```

```elixir
location = %{
  lat: Kino.Input.read(lat_in),
  lon: Kino.Input.read(lon_in),
  height: Kino.Input.read(height_in)
}

datetime = read_utc.(utc_in)
bodies = [:sun, :moon, :mercury, :venus, :mars, :jupiter, :saturn]

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**Inputs locked**

| param | value |
|-------|-------|
| datetime | `#{DateTime.to_iso8601(datetime)}` |
| location | `#{location.lat}°, #{location.lon}°` @ #{location.height} m |
| bodies | `#{inspect(bodies)}` |
""")
```

## 2 — Like-for-like alignment

What this notebook compares (and what it deliberately does *not*):

* Shared `de440s.bsp`; outer planets are barycenters.
* **Geometric** lon/lat/RA/Dec/rate: `observe/4` vs Skyfield astrometric
  (expect residual ″ under Mix's ~72″ gate — not identically zero).
* **Apparent** alt/az: `ApparentPlace.look/2` vs Skyfield `.apparent().altaz()`
  — **not** `obs.bodies` (those stay geometric even with correction flags).
* Correction flags on `observe/4` only fill apparent *geocentric* lon/lat;
  they do not change pipeline alt/az.

## 3 — Python env (`uv` + Skyfield) — optional

This notebook is **optional**. It needs [Pythonx](https://hexdocs.pm/pythonx) to
embed CPython in the BEAM. First evaluation downloads CPython + Skyfield via `uv`
into Pythonx's cache (network required once). Re-runs are fast. If Pythonx init
fails, skip §4–§5 — the pipeline tour in
[`01_snapshot_pipeline.livemd`](../01_observe_pipeline/01_snapshot_pipeline.livemd) needs no Python.

```elixir
kernel_path =
  EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.spk_path("de440s.bsp")

pythonx_ok? =
  try do
    # native_tls: true helps when Erlang's TLS stack rejects GitHub/uv certs
    # (corporate proxies, some macOS setups). Harmless otherwise.
    Pythonx.uv_init(
      """
      [project]
      name = "eph-core-snapshot-skyfield"
      version = "0.0.0"
      requires-python = ">=3.11"
      dependencies = [
        "skyfield>=1.49",
        "numpy>=1.24"
      ]
      """,
      native_tls: true
    )

    true
  rescue
    e ->
      Kino.render(
        Kino.Markdown.new("""
        **Pythonx init failed** — `#{Exception.message(e)}`

        Need a network on first run (`uv` fetches CPython + Skyfield). Then re-evaluate.
        If the error is a TLS / certificate alert fetching `uv`, try again or install
        `uv` manually; Pythonx caches it under `~/Library/Caches/pythonx/` (macOS).
        """)
      )

      false
  end

Kino.Markdown.new(
  if pythonx_ok? do
    "✅ Pythonx ready · kernel `#{kernel_path}`"
  else
    "❌ Pythonx not ready — fix §3 before §4/§5"
  end
)
```

## 4 — Warm load (not timed)

Load the BSP + timescale into Python globals and run one Elixir observe so
neither side pays cold-start in the timed cell.

```elixir
sf_bodies = bodies
body_names = Enum.map(sf_bodies, &Atom.to_string/1)

{py_warm, py_globals} =
  if pythonx_ok? do
    Pythonx.eval(
      """
      from skyfield.api import load, wgs84
      from skyfield.framelib import ecliptic_frame

      # Pythonx encodes Elixir binaries as Python bytes — decode paths/names.
      def _s(x):
          return x.decode() if isinstance(x, (bytes, bytearray)) else x

      BODY_MAP = {
          "sun": "sun",
          "moon": "moon",
          "mercury": "mercury",
          "venus": "venus",
          "mars": "mars barycenter",
          "jupiter": "jupiter barycenter",
          "saturn": "saturn barycenter",
          "uranus": "uranus barycenter",
          "neptune": "neptune barycenter",
          "pluto": "pluto barycenter",
      }

      ts = load.timescale()
      planets = load(_s(kernel_path))
      earth = planets["earth"]
      "warm"
      """,
      %{"kernel_path" => kernel_path}
    )
  else
    {nil, %{}}
  end

_ =
  if pythonx_ok? do
    EphCore.SnapshotPipeline.observe(datetime, location, sf_bodies,
      models: %{earth_orientation: :gast}
    )
  end

Kino.Markdown.new(
  if pythonx_ok? do
    "Warmed · Python `#{inspect(Pythonx.decode(py_warm))}` · Elixir observe once · bodies `#{inspect(body_names)}`"
  else
    "Skipped warm — Pythonx not ready"
  end
)
```

## 5 — Timed compute + diffs

```elixir
angle_delta_deg = fn a, b ->
  d = abs(a - b)
  min(d, 360.0 - d)
end

skyfield_compute = fn globals ->
  Pythonx.eval(
    """
    def _s(x):
        return x.decode() if isinstance(x, (bytes, bytearray)) else x

    t = ts.utc(year, month, day, hour, minute, second)
    dt_days = motion_dt_minutes / 1440.0
    # Vectorize t_prev / t / t_next — Skyfield's natural fast path for rates.
    t3 = ts.tt_jd([t.tt - dt_days, t.tt, t.tt + dt_days])
    observer = wgs84.latlon(lat, lon, elevation_m=height)
    topos = earth + observer
    out = {}
    for name in bodies:
        key = _s(name)
        target = planets[BODY_MAP[key]]
        geo3 = earth.at(t3).observe(target)
        geo = geo3[1]
        app = topos.at(t).observe(target).apparent()
        ra, dec, _ = geo.radec()
        alt, az, _ = app.altaz()
        lat_tod, lon_tod, _ = geo.frame_latlon(ecliptic_frame)
        lon_prev = float(geo3[0].frame_latlon(ecliptic_frame)[1].degrees)
        lon_next = float(geo3[2].frame_latlon(ecliptic_frame)[1].degrees)
        delta = lon_next - lon_prev
        if delta > 180.0:
            delta -= 360.0
        elif delta < -180.0:
            delta += 360.0
        rate = float(delta * (720.0 / motion_dt_minutes))
        out[key] = {
            "ra": float(ra._degrees),
            "dec": float(dec.degrees),
            "alt_apparent": float(alt.degrees),
            "az_apparent": float(az.degrees),
            "lon_tod": float(lon_tod.degrees),
            "lat_tod": float(lat_tod.degrees),
            "rate": rate,
            "retrograde": bool(rate < 0),
        }
    out
    """,
    Map.merge(globals, %{
      "year" => datetime.year,
      "month" => datetime.month,
      "day" => datetime.day,
      "hour" => datetime.hour,
      "minute" => datetime.minute,
      "second" => datetime.second,
      "lat" => location.lat * 1.0,
      "lon" => location.lon * 1.0,
      "height" => location.height * 1.0,
      "bodies" => body_names,
      "motion_dt_minutes" => 30
    })
  )
end

if pythonx_ok? do
  alias EphCore.Corrections.ApparentPlace

  # One more Python touch so the timed pass isn't the first eval after warm.
  {_prime, py_globals} = skyfield_compute.(py_globals)

  # Skyfield's timed compute() already runs topos.at(t).observe(target).apparent()
  # per body (light-time + aberration + alt/az), so a fair Elixir comparison must
  # pay for the same apparent transform inside its own timed block — not call
  # ApparentPlace.look/2 afterward, uncharged. This "matched" pass runs observe/4
  # AND the per-body apparent look together, timed once at single-core (fair vs
  # Skyfield's sequential body loop) and once at Elixir's default multi-core
  # fan-out (reported separately — it's a concurrency bonus, not a language
  # speed claim).
  matched_pass = fn parallel? ->
    {:ok, obs} =
      EphCore.SnapshotPipeline.observe(datetime, location, sf_bodies,
        models: %{earth_orientation: :gast},
        parallel: parallel?
      )

    frame = ApparentPlace.frame(datetime, location)
    _looks = for body <- sf_bodies, do: ApparentPlace.look(frame, body)
    obs
  end

  _ = matched_pass.(false)
  _ = matched_pass.(true)

  {elixir_single_us, _} = :timer.tc(fn -> matched_pass.(false) end)
  {elixir_multi_us, {:ok, obs_sf}} = :timer.tc(fn -> {:ok, matched_pass.(true)} end)

  {skyfield_us, {sf_result, _py_globals}} = :timer.tc(fn -> skyfield_compute.(py_globals) end)
  sf_bodies_map = Pythonx.decode(sf_result)

  # Apparent topocentric look — like-for-like with Skyfield .apparent().altaz().
  ap_frame = ApparentPlace.frame(datetime, location)

  geo_rows =
    for body <- sf_bodies do
      name = Atom.to_string(body)
      ssp = obs_sf.solar_system_positions[body]
      motion = obs_sf.motion[body]
      sfb = sf_bodies_map[name]

      lon_d = angle_delta_deg.(ssp.ecliptic_longitude, sfb["lon_tod"])
      lat_d = abs(ssp.ecliptic_latitude - sfb["lat_tod"])
      ra_d = angle_delta_deg.(ssp.right_ascension, sfb["ra"])
      dec_d = abs(ssp.declination - sfb["dec"])
      rate_d = abs(motion.ecliptic_lon_rate_deg_per_day - sfb["rate"])

      %{
        body: body,
        d_lon_arcsec: Float.round(lon_d * 3600, 2),
        d_lat_arcsec: Float.round(lat_d * 3600, 2),
        d_ra_arcsec: Float.round(ra_d * 3600, 2),
        d_dec_arcsec: Float.round(dec_d * 3600, 2),
        d_rate_deg_day: Float.round(rate_d, 5),
        retrograde_match: motion.retrograde == sfb["retrograde"]
      }
    end

  apparent_rows =
    for body <- sf_bodies do
      name = Atom.to_string(body)
      look = ApparentPlace.look(ap_frame, body)
      sfb = sf_bodies_map[name]
      alt_d = abs(look.alt_deg - sfb["alt_apparent"])
      az_d = angle_delta_deg.(look.az_deg, sfb["az_apparent"])

      %{
        body: body,
        d_alt_arcmin: Float.round(alt_d * 60, 3),
        d_az_arcmin: Float.round(az_d * 60, 3)
      }
    end

  timing_rows = [
    %{
      engine: "EphCore matched (observe/4 + ApparentPlace.look/2), single-core",
      wall_ms: Float.round(elixir_single_us / 1000, 2),
      notes: "fair vs Skyfield's sequential per-body loop"
    },
    %{
      engine: "EphCore matched (observe/4 + ApparentPlace.look/2), multi-core (default)",
      wall_ms: Float.round(elixir_multi_us / 1000, 2),
      notes: "Elixir's free concurrency — separate axis, not a language-speed claim"
    },
    %{
      engine: "Skyfield (Pythonx)",
      wall_ms: Float.round(skyfield_us / 1000, 2),
      notes: "in-process CPython; kernel warm; vectorized t±dt; incl. .apparent().altaz()"
    }
  ]

  Kino.render(Kino.DataTable.new(timing_rows, name: "One-shot wall time (warm compute, matched physics)"))

  Kino.render(
    Kino.Markdown.new("""
    **Epoch:** `#{DateTime.to_iso8601(datetime)}` @ #{location.lat}°, #{location.lon}°
    **Runtime:** Pythonx + `uv` (Skyfield ≥ 1.49) · shared `de440s.bsp`

    Both engines now pay for the same physics in the timed pass: light-time +
    aberration + apparent alt/az (Skyfield's `.apparent().altaz()`, Elixir's
    `observe/4` + `ApparentPlace.look/2`). Fair ratio is single-core vs
    single-core; the multi-core row is Elixir's default fan-out reported
    separately since a 7-body job is too small for a Python dev to reach for
    `multiprocessing`/threads (spawn/GIL overhead would dominate).

    Geometric table: `observe/4` vs Skyfield astrometric (″). Apparent table:
    `ApparentPlace.look/2` vs Skyfield `.apparent()` (′). Mix gates ≈ 72″ / 0.6°.
    """)
  )

  Kino.render(Kino.DataTable.new(geo_rows, name: "Geometric vs geometric (astrometric)"))
  Kino.DataTable.new(apparent_rows, name: "Apparent vs apparent alt/az (ApparentPlace.look)")
else
  Kino.Markdown.new("Skipped timed compare — re-evaluate **§3** successfully first.")
end
```

